hing in the available evidence — no polls, ratings, or spending reports — contradicts it. A national climate slightly favorable to Democrats trims the expected Republican margin by a point, far short of putting the state in play.", "watch": "A decisive national Democratic wave or an unusually strong Democratic ticket could narrow the margin, though a Wyoming win would still require an unprecedented shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Wyoming remains one of the most Republican states in the country, with a partisan lean of roughly 23 points toward the GOP and no competitive general-election history at the presidential level. The incumbent president is term-limited, making this an open contest, but the state's partisan makeup points strongly to the Republican ticket.\n\nNo public polls of the race or expert ratings are available, and neither side has reported fundraising figures. National headlines suggest a political environment modestly favorable to Democrats, which may trim the Republican margin by a point or two, but nothing in the record indicates Wyoming is in play. The Republican nominee is a heavy favorite, and the race is rated Safe Republican, with wide uncertainty given the absence of state polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.01, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0232, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0282, "margin": -22.0}]}